// Preview servers.
//
// Open the browser pane and the AI starts these, then verifies its own changes
// against the RUNNING APP rather than against a green typecheck. That is the
// difference between "it compiles" and "it works".
//
// COMMITTED DELIBERATELY, so it works for everyone on first connect. The point
// of the engagement is that the client builds here instead of paying a metered
// agent — and that fails on friction, not on features. If the app does not start
// on the first attempt, they go back to the platform and the bill returns.
//
// 🔴 THE ORDER MATTERS. The frontend proxies /api to the backend, so the backend
//    starts first. Check the frontend can actually REACH the API in dev: in
//    production these apps usually serve the API and the built frontend from one
//    origin, so the generated client uses relative paths and sets no base URL.
//    Run the frontend on its own port without a proxy entry in the bundler
//    config and EVERY API call 404s.
//
// 🔴 NEVER PUT SECRETS HERE — this file is committed. Real values live in .env,
//    which is gitignored. See .env.example and GENERAL-secrets.md.
//
// 🔴 WORKERS_ENABLED=false. This is not the host. See .env.example, item 1.
{
  "version": "0.0.1",
  "configurations": [
    {
      "name": "api",
      "runtimeExecutable": "pnpm",
      "runtimeArgs": ["--filter", "TODO-api-package", "dev"],
      "port": 5000,
      "env": {
        "NODE_ENV": "development",
        "PORT": "5000",
        "WORKERS_ENABLED": "false",
        "AUTH_DEV_BYPASS": "1"
      }
    },
    {
      "name": "web",
      "runtimeExecutable": "pnpm",
      "runtimeArgs": ["--filter", "TODO-frontend-package", "dev"],
      "port": 5173,
      "env": {
        "PORT": "5173",
        "API_PROXY_TARGET": "http://127.0.0.1:5000"
      }
    }
  ]
}
